  <!-- FAQ (portfolio & risk) -->
  <Section
    heading="Common questions"
    align="center"
  >
    <div id="pt-faq">
    <dl class="faq-accordion max-w-2xl mx-auto space-y-1 text-left" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
      <div
        class="faq-item border-b border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800"
        itemscope
        itemprop="mainEntity"
        itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"
      >
        <dt class="faq-question">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="faq-trigger w-full flex items-center justify-between gap-4 text-left py-5 text-base font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 transition-colors"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="faq-answer-1"
            id="faq-question-1"
          >
            <span itemprop="name">How do you calculate risk-adjusted returns, and why is it better than just looking at total returns?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon flex-shrink-0 w-4 h-4 text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500 transition-transform duration-200" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4"/>
              </svg>
            </span>
          </button>
        </dt>
        <dd id="faq-answer-1" class="faq-answer" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-question-1" itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner pb-6 pt-0" itemprop="text">
            <p class="text-base text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
              Risk-adjusted returns measure how much return you earned relative to the volatility (risk) you took to get there. A portfolio that returned 15% with low volatility is often better than one that returned 20% with massive swings, especially if those swings would have caused you to panic-sell at the bottom. The most common metric is the Sharpe ratio: (return minus risk-free rate) divided by volatility. A higher Sharpe means you're earning more return per unit of risk. Two portfolios with identical returns can have very different risk profiles. One might be a steady climb, the other a white-knuckle rollercoaster. Risk-adjusted metrics reveal which strategy is actually superior.
            </p>
          </div>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div
        class="faq-item border-b border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800"
        itemscope
        itemprop="mainEntity"
        itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"
      >
        <dt class="faq-question">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="faq-trigger w-full flex items-center justify-between gap-4 text-left py-5 text-base font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 transition-colors"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="faq-answer-2"
            id="faq-question-2"
          >
            <span itemprop="name">What's a healthy asset allocation for a diversified multi-asset portfolio?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon flex-shrink-0 w-4 h-4 text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500 transition-transform duration-200" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4"/>
              </svg>
            </span>
          </button>
        </dt>
        <dd id="faq-answer-2" class="faq-answer" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-question-2" itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner pb-6 pt-0" itemprop="text">
            <p class="text-base text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
              There's no one-size-fits-all answer. It depends on your age, risk tolerance, time horizon, and financial goals. Traditional advice suggests stocks/bonds ratios based on age (e.g., "110 minus your age" in stocks), but modern portfolios often include crypto (5-10% for aggressive investors), commodities (5-15% as inflation hedges), and alternative assets. The key principle: hold asset classes that don't move in perfect correlation. When stocks fall, commodities or forex might rise. Younger investors can tolerate more volatility and might go 60-70% equities, 10-15% crypto, 10-15% commodities, 5-10% cash. Older investors might prefer 40% stocks, 30% bonds, 20% commodities, 10% cash. Regular rebalancing (quarterly or semi-annually) maintains your target allocation as different assets outperform.
            </p>
          </div>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div
        class="faq-item border-b border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800"
        itemscope
        itemprop="mainEntity"
        itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"
      >
        <dt class="faq-question">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="faq-trigger w-full flex items-center justify-between gap-4 text-left py-5 text-base font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 transition-colors"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="faq-answer-3"
            id="faq-question-3"
          >
            <span itemprop="name">What's annualized volatility and how does it help me understand my portfolio risk?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon flex-shrink-0 w-4 h-4 text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500 transition-transform duration-200" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4"/>
              </svg>
            </span>
          </button>
        </dt>
        <dd id="faq-answer-3" class="faq-answer" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-question-3" itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner pb-6 pt-0" itemprop="text">
            <p class="text-base text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
              Annualized volatility measures how much your portfolio value fluctuates over time, expressed as a percentage. A portfolio with 15% annualized volatility means you can expect the value to swing up or down by roughly 15% in a typical year. Low volatility (5-10%) suggests stable, predictable returns, think bonds or dividend stocks. High volatility (30-50%+) means wild swings, common with crypto or individual growth stocks. Knowing your portfolio's volatility helps you: (1) assess if you can stomach the ups and downs psychologically, (2) compare different investment strategies on a risk-adjusted basis, and (3) size positions appropriately. A volatile asset shouldn't dominate your portfolio. Volatility isn't inherently bad, but you need to know what you're signing up for.
            </p>
          </div>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div
        class="faq-item border-b border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800"
        itemscope
        itemprop="mainEntity"
        itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"
      >
        <dt class="faq-question">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="faq-trigger w-full flex items-center justify-between gap-4 text-left py-5 text-base font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 transition-colors"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="faq-answer-4"
            id="faq-question-4"
          >
            <span itemprop="name">What's portfolio concentration risk and how much is too much in one asset?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon flex-shrink-0 w-4 h-4 text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500 transition-transform duration-200" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4"/>
              </svg>
            </span>
          </button>
        </dt>
        <dd id="faq-answer-4" class="faq-answer" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-question-4" itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner pb-6 pt-0" itemprop="text">
            <p class="text-base text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
              Concentration risk is when a single asset, sector, or asset class dominates your portfolio. If it crashes, you're in trouble. Classic example: Enron employees with 60% of retirement in company stock. In multi-asset portfolios, concentration often creeps in unintentionally. Maybe crypto boomed and now represents 40% of your net worth even though you only invested 10%. General guidelines: no single stock should exceed 5-10% of your portfolio, no single asset class should exceed 50-60% (except for very young, aggressive investors), and no single sector should dominate. Geographic concentration also matters. Don't put everything in your home country. The right concentration level depends on risk tolerance and time horizon, but if losing 50% of one position would derail your financial plans, you're too concentrated.
            </p>
          </div>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <script is:inline>
      document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
        var accordion = document.querySelector('#pt-faq .faq-accordion');
        if (!accordion) return;
        var items = accordion.querySelectorAll('.faq-item');
        var openClass = 'faq-item-open';

        function closeAll() {
          items.forEach(function (item) {
            item.classList.remove(openClass);
            var btn = item.querySelector('.faq-trigger');
            if (btn) btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
          });
        }

        function openItem(item) {
          closeAll();
          item.classList.add(openClass);
          var btn = item.querySelector('.faq-trigger');
          if (btn) btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'true');
        }

        function toggleItem(item) {
          var isOpen = item.classList.contains(openClass);
          if (isOpen) closeAll();
          else openItem(item);
        }

        items.forEach(function (item) {
          var btn = item.querySelector('.faq-trigger');
          if (!btn) return;
          btn.addEventListener('click', function () { toggleItem(item); });
        });
      });
    </script>
    </div>
  </Section>

